{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>When you enter, etc. and you shall say, etc. you shall surely set, etc.</b> From His saying <b>when you enter</b>, etc., <b>and you shall say</b>, it implies that it is not a commandment of HaShem upon them to appoint a king, but rather if they say so, the permission is in their hand. And from the fact that He concluded by saying <b>you shall surely set</b> (som tasim), the measure of the language proves that it is a commandment of HaShem to appoint over them a king." "<b>And it appears</b> that the intent of the verse is along this path, for there are two categories in the appointment of a king: the first is that the trust be in him to conduct the wars by the force of his wisdom and to go forth as a mighty warrior on the day of battle according to the order of the kings of the nations — and this is a thing hateful in the eyes of HaShem, for the order of the kings of the nations does not follow the quality of deed but rather after physical utilities; even if nothing better than what is intellectually grasped is found in him, still they place their trust and hope in him, while from HaShem their heart is turned away. And the second category is for the honor and glory of Israel, and so that HaShem may act through his merit in the manner that HaShem used to save Israel by the hand of the judges and by the hand of the worthy kings of Israel — go forth and learn from King David, etc. — and this matter is grace and good understanding in the eyes of Elohim and man." "<b>And therefore</b> His good word comes here and says <b>when you enter</b>, etc., <b>and you shall say: I will set over me a king like all the nations</b>, etc. — and this is the first category we mentioned. And He concluded by saying <b>you shall surely set</b>, etc. — meaning, this evil thing shall not arise, but rather the appointing that you shall set is along this path: whom HaShem your Elohim shall choose in him. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tosefta Sanhedrin ch. 3), and this is their language: they do not appoint a king initially except by the ruling of a court of seventy elders and by the word of a prophet, etc. — end quote. And a king appointed in this order is not like the order of a king appointed among the nations, as it says in their words: ״like all the nations.״ Accordingly, the statement <b>you shall surely set</b> is a commandment and a negation of the category that runs counter to its conditions. And therefore HaShem was wise to preface the statement <b>and you shall say: I will set</b>, etc., to alert you to what is intended, so as to negate it through the statement ״you shall surely set,״ etc. — for without this I would not have understood that it is forbidden to set a king like all the nations; and even though it says ״whom [HaShem] shall choose,״ it is not self-evident that this comes to prohibit like all the nations, for it could be both this and that, and one would not be careful to understand in the statement ״you shall surely set״ that it is mere permission because of the doubling ״שׂוֹם תָּשִׂים.״" "<b>And you will find</b> that HaShem was displeased with Israel when they asked in the days of Samuel to give them a king (I Samuel 8:5). And the matter is difficult: why would HaShem be displeased with them? For even if the intent of HaShem in this verse were mere permission and not a commandment, the permission is still granted. But according to what we have written there is room for HaShem to be displeased with the matter, since HaShem commanded them never to say they would set a king like all the nations, as we explained; and yet they asked for a king and said (I Samuel 8:5) ״to judge us like all the nations״ — therefore HaShem was displeased. But had they asked for themselves a king according to the will of the Blessed One — not for power and might in the way of the nations — they would have been fulfilling a positive commandment." "<b>And I saw</b> in Sanhedrin, chapter ״Kohen Gadol״ (Sanhedrin 20b) that our Rabbis of blessed memory disagreed, and this is its language: Rabbi Yehudah says: Israel was commanded three commandments upon their entry into the Land — to appoint for themselves a king, and to eradicate the seed of Amalek, and to build the Chosen House. Rabbi Nehorai says: this passage was stated only against their murmuring, as it is said ״and you shall say: I will set over me a king,״ etc. — end quote. And our words align with the words of Rabbi Yehudah and not with the words of Rabbi Nehorai. And even though the halachah follows Rabbi Yehudah, and so the Rambam ruled in chapter 1 of the Laws of Kings, nevertheless one must reconcile the verse according to the reasoning of Rabbi Nehorai, since the plain sense of His saying <b>you shall surely set</b> declares that it is a commandment of HaShem to set a king." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that if Israel are worthy and do not ask for a king, then HaShem would be king over them, in the manner of His saying (I Samuel 12:13) ״וַה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם מַלְכְּכֶם״ — and HaShem too desires to be the king of Israel, He alone. But if Israel enter the category of asking, desiring a king over themselves, then the commandment of the king is upon them to fulfill, as He says <b>you shall surely set</b>. And therefore Scripture did not say <b>you shall surely set</b> until after the statement <b>and you shall say: I shall set</b> etc. And according to this, even according to the opinion of Rabbi Nehorai one may explain <b>you shall surely set</b> as a commandment only after they say ״I shall set״ etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {1 "The phrase ומן ה׳ יסור לבבם is rendered hyper-literally; the construction is textually compressed and may carry an elipsis."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:12.003814539Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}